===Verb===
{{en-verb|draws|drawing|drew|drawn|past_ptc2=drew|past_ptc2_qual=colloquial and nonstandard}}

# ''To move or develop something.''
## To [[sketch]]; depict with lines; to [[produce]] a [[picture]] with [[pencil]], [[crayon]], [[chalk]], etc. on [[paper]], [[cardboard]], etc.
##* {{RQ:Goldsmith Retaliation|page=10|passage=A flattering painter, vvho made it his care / To '''dravv''' men as they ought to be, not as they are.}}
##* {{RQ:Prior Poems|year=1718|poem=To Mr. Howard|page=70|stanza=I|passage=Can I untouch'd the Fair ones Paſſions move? / Or Thou '''draw''' Beauty, and not feel it's Pow'r?}}
##* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=III|passage=Sepia Delft tiles surrounded the fireplace, their crudely '''drawn''' Biblical scenes in faded cyclamen blending with the pinkish pine, while above them, instead of a mantelshelf, there was an archway high enough to form a balcony with slender balusters and a tapestry-hung wall behind.}}
## To [[deduce]] or [[infer]].
##: {{ux|en|He tried to '''draw''' a conclusion from the facts.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|transitive|of drinks, especially [[tea]]}} To [[steep]], leave temporarily so as to allow the [[flavour]] to increase.
##* {{RQ:Lindsay Redheap|page=207|passage="There's your tay set for you an' '''drawin'''' nicely this minute, Miss Ethel," called old Bridget from the hall.}}
##: {{ux|en|Tea is much nicer if you let it '''draw''' for more than two minutes before pouring.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To take or [[procure]] from a place of [[deposit]]; to call for and receive from a [[fund]], etc.
##: {{ux|en|to '''draw''' money from a bank}}
## To take into the lungs; to [[inhale]].
##* {{RQ:Hough Purchase Price|chapter=I|passage=Serene, smiling, enigmatic, she faced him with no fear whatever showing in her dark eyes.{{...}}She put back a truant curl from her forehead where it had sought egress to the world, and looked him full in the face now, '''drawing''' a deep breath which caused the round of her bosom to lift the lace at her throat.}}
##* '''1979''', {{w|Monty Python}}, ''{{w|Always Look on the Bright Side of Life}}''
##*: So always look on the bright side of death / Just before you '''draw''' your terminal breath
## {{lb|en|used with prepositions and adverbs}} To move; to come or go.
##: {{ux|en|We '''drew''' back from the cliff edge.}}
##: {{ux|en|The runners '''drew''' level with each other as they approached the finish line.}}
##: {{ux|en|'''Draw''' near to the fire and I will tell you a tale.}}
## To approach, come to, or arrive at a point in time or a process.
##: {{ux|en|The end of the world '''draws''' near.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1962|month=October|title=The Victoria Line was only part of the plan|journal=Modern Railways|page=258|text=As the war '''drew''' to its end, it became evident that repairs and rebuilding in the heavily blitzed Greater London area would be so extensive as to afford opportunity for effective large-scale planning.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To obtain from some cause or origin; to infer from evidence or reasons; to deduce from premises; to derive.
##* {{RQ:Burke Revolution in France|passage=We do not '''draw''' the moral lessons we might from history.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[withdraw]].
##*{{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-2|act=II|scene=i|passage=Go, wash thy face, and '''draw''' thy action.}}
## {{lb|en|archaic}} To [[draw up]] (a document).
##: {{ux|en|to '''draw''' a memorial, a deed, or bill of exchange}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|act=IV|scene=i|passage=Clerk, '''draw''' a deed of gift.}}
# ''To exert or experience force.''
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[drag]], [[pull]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1913|author={{w|Robert Barr (writer)|Robert Barr}}|chapter=4|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL1796924W Lord Stranleigh Abroad]|passage=“[…] No rogue e’er felt the halter '''draw''', with a good opinion of the law, and perhaps my own detestation of the law arises from my having frequently broken it.{{nb...}}”}}
##* {{RQ:Burroughs Land That Time Forgot|chapter=VIII|passage=Lys shuddered, and I put my arm around her and '''drew''' her to me; and thus we sat throughout the hot night. She told me of her abduction and of the fright she had undergone, and together we thanked God that she had come through unharmed, because the great brute had dared not pause along the danger-infested way.}}
##* {{RQ:Orwell Animal Farm|chapter=1|passage=At the last moment Mollie, the foolish, pretty white mare who '''drew''' Mr. Jones's trap, came mincing daintily in, chewing at a lump of sugar.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To pull; to exert strength in drawing anything; to have force to move anything by pulling.
##: {{ux|en|This horse '''draws''' well.}}
##: {{ux|en|A ship's sail is said to '''draw''' when it is filled with wind.}}
## To [[pull out]], [[unsheathe]] (as a [[gun]] from a [[holster]], or a [[tooth]]).
##: {{ux|en|They '''drew''' their swords and fought each other.}}
## To undergo the action of pulling or dragging.
##: {{ux|en|The carriage '''draws''' easily.}}
## {{lb|en|archery}} To pull back the [[bowstring]] and its [[arrow]] in preparation for shooting.
## {{lb|en|of curtains, etc.}} To [[close]].
##: {{ux|en|You should '''draw''' the curtains at night.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1944|month=November and December|title="Duplex Roomette" Sleeping Cars|journal=Railway Magazine|page=324|text=It is realised that the old Pullman standard sleeper, with its convertible "sections", each containing upper and lower berths, and with no greater privacy at night than the curtains '''drawn''' along both sides of a middle aisle, has had its day.}}
## {{lb|en|of curtains, etc.}} To [[open]].
##: {{ux|en|She '''drew''' the curtains to let in the sunlight.}}
## {{lb|en|cards}} To take the top card of a deck into hand.
##: {{ux|en|At the start of their turn, each player must '''draw''' a card.}}
# {{lb|en|fluidic}} ''To remove or separate or displace.''
## To extract a liquid, or cause a liquid to come out, primarily water or blood.
##: {{ux|en|'''draw''' water from a well;&emsp; '''draw''' water for a bath;&emsp; the wound '''drew''' blood}}
##* {{RQ:King James Version|John|4|11|passage=The woman saith unto him, Sir, thou hast nothing to '''draw''' with, and the well is deep.}}
##* '''1705''', {{w|George Cheyne (physician)|George Cheyne}}, ''Philosophical Principles of Religion Natural and Revealed''
##*: Spirits, by distillations, may be '''drawn''' out of vegetable juices, which shall flame and fume of themselves.
## To drain by emptying; to suck dry.
##* '''1705''', [http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/8943627 Richard Wiseman], ''[https://books.google.com/books?id=P5EIAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA303 Tumours, Gun Shot Wounds, &amp;c.]''
##*: Sucking and '''drawing''' the breast dischargeth the milk as fast as it can be generated.
## {{lb|en|figurative}} To [[extract]]; to [[force]] out; to [[elicit]]; to [[derive]].
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Winter's Tale|act=I|scene=ii|passage=I had thought, sir, to have held my peace until<br>You have '''drawn''' oaths from him not to stay.}}
## To sink in water; to require a depth for floating.
##: {{ux|en|A ship '''draws''' ten feet of water.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Troilus and Cressida|act=II|scene=iii|passage=Greater hulks '''draw''' deep.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|medicine|dated}} To work as an [[epispastic]]; said of a blister, poultice, etc.
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To have a [[draught]]; to transmit smoke, gases, etc.
##: {{ux|en|The chimney won't '''draw''' properly if it's clogged up with soot.}}
## {{lb|en|analogous}} To consume, for example, [[power]].
##: {{ux|en|The circuit '''draws''' three hundred watts.}}
# ''To change in size or shape.''
## To [[extend]] in length; to [[lengthen]]; to [[protract]]; to [[stretch]].
##: {{ux|en|to '''draw''' a mass of metal into wire}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 8|act=IV|scene=ii|passage=How long her face is '''drawn'''!}}
##* '''1874''', {{w|John Richard Green}}, ''{{w|A Short History of the English People}}''
##*: the huge Offa's dike which he '''drew''' from the mouth of Wye to that of Dee
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become [[contracted]]; to [[shrink]].
##* {{RQ:Bacon Sylva Sylvarum|passage=water{{...}} will shrink or '''draw''' into less room}}
# ''To attract or be attracted.''
## To [[attract]].
##: {{ux|en|The citizens were afraid the casino would '''draw''' an undesirable element to their town.}}
##: {{ux|en|I was '''drawn''' to her.}}
##* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=5|passage=When you're well enough off so's you don't have to fret about anything but your heft or your diseases you begin to get queer, I suppose. And the queerer the cure for those ailings the bigger the attraction. A place like the Right Livers' Rest was bound to '''draw''' freaks, same as molasses '''draws''' flies.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1935|author=[https://www.goodreads.com/author/show/288354.George_Goodchild George Goodchild]|title=Death on the Centre Court|chapter=5|passage=By one o'clock the place was choc-a-bloc. […] The restaurant was packed, and the promenade between the two main courts and the subsidiary courts was thronged with healthy-looking youngish people, '''drawn''' to the Mecca of tennis from all parts of the country.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1964|month=April|title=Letters: Rethinking emergency procedures|journal=Modern Railways|page=274|text={{...}} 1. Handsignalmen, where needed, ought to wear a conspicuous orange/yellow cape (like many road workmen) to '''draw''' attention to them.}}
## To induce (a [[reticent]] person) to speak.
##: {{ux|en|He refused to be '''drawn''' on the subject}}
## {{lb|en|hunting}} To [[search]] for game.
##* '''1928''', {{w|Siegfried Sassoon}}, ''{{w|Memoirs of a Fox-Hunting Man}}'', Penguin 2013, p.87:
##*: On one of my expeditions, after a stormy night, at the end of March, the hounds '''drew''' all day without finding a fox.
## To [[cause]].
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=July 3, 2011|author=Piers Newbury|work=BBC Sport|title=[http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/tennis/14006575.stm Wimbledon 2011: Novak Djokovic beats Rafael Nadal in final]|passage=In a desperately tight opening set, the pace and accuracy of the Serbian's groundstrokes began to '''draw''' errors from the usually faultless Nadal and earned him the first break point of the day at 5-4.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To exert an attractive force; {{lb|en|figurative}} to act as an inducement or enticement.
##* {{RQ:Bacon Sylva Sylvarum|passage=These following bodies do not '''draw''': smaragd, achates, corneolus, pearl, jaspis, chalcedonius, alabaster, porphyry, coral, marble, touchstone, haematites, or bloodstone {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Spectator|II|128|27 July 1711|passage=Keep a watch upon the particular bias which nature has fixed in their minds, that it may not '''draw''' too much.}}
# {{lb|en|usually as ''[[draw on]]'' or ''draw upon''}} To rely on; [[utilize]] as a source.
#: {{ux|en|She had to '''draw''' upon her experience to solve the problem.}}
#* '''January 19 1782''', {{w|Benjamin Franklin}}, ''letter to {{w|John Jay}}''
#*: but I would have you '''draw''' on me for a Quarter at present which shall be paid
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2012|month=March-April|author=John T. Jost
|volume=100|issue=2|page=162|magazine={{w|American Scientist}}
|title=[http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/social-justice-is-it-in-our-nature-and-our-future Social Justice: Is It in Our Nature (and Our Future)?]
|passage=He '''draws''' eclectically on studies of baboons, descriptive anthropological accounts of hunter-gatherer societies and, in a few cases, the fossil record.}}
# To [[disembowel]].
#* '''1709''', {{w|William King (poet)|William King}}, ''The Art of Cookery''
#*: In private '''draw''' your poultry, clean your tripe.
# {{lb|en|transitive|or|intransitive}} To end a game in a [[#Noun|draw]] (with neither side winning).
#: {{ux|en|We '''drew''' last time we played.&emsp; I '''drew''' him last time I played him.&emsp; I '''drew''' my last game against him.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1922|year_published='''2010'''|edition=HTML|author={{w|Edgar Rice Burroughs}}|title=[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/1153/1153-h/1153-h.htm The Chessmen of Mars]|publisher=The Gutenberg Project|passage=The game is won when a player places any of his pieces on the same square with his opponent's Princess, or when a Chief takes a Chief. It is '''drawn''' when a Chief is taken by any opposing piece other than the opposing Chief;{{nb...}}}}
# ''To choose by means of a random selection process.''
## To select by the drawing of lots.
##: {{ux|en|The winning lottery numbers were '''drawn''' every Tuesday.}}
##* '''1784''', {{w|Edward Augustus Freeman}}, [https://archive.org/details/essayonparliamen00edinuoft An essay on parliamentary representation, and the magistracies of our boroughs royal:{{nb...}}]
##*: Provided magistracies were filled by men freely chosen or '''drawn'''.
##* '''1859''', {{w|Charles Dickens}}, ''The Haunted House''
##*: In the drawing of lots, my sister '''drew''' her own room, and I drew Master B.'s.
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To win in a [[lottery]] or similar game of chance.
##: {{ux|en|He '''drew''' a prize.}}
## {{lb|en|poker}} To trade in cards for replacements in [[draw poker]] games; to attempt to improve one's hand with future cards. ''See also'' '''[[draw out]]'''.
##: {{ux|en|Jill has four diamonds; she'll try to '''draw''' for a flush.}}
# {{lb|en|curling}} To make a shot that lands gently in the [[house]] (the circular target) without knocking out other [[stone]]s.
# {{lb|en|cricket}} To play (a short-length ball directed at the leg stump) with an inclined bat so as to deflect the ball between the legs and the wicket.
# {{lb|en|golf}} To hit (the ball) with the toe of the club so that it is deflected toward the left.
# {{lb|en|billiards}} To strike (the cue ball) below the center so as to give it a backward rotation which causes it to take a backward direction on striking another ball.

